IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Civil Appeal No. 7192 of 2021
CITATION: (2022) 5 SCC 1
YEAR: 2022

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: L. Nageswara Rao, B.R. Gavai, B.V. Nagarathna

PARTIES:
Renaissance Hotel vs B. Vijaya Sai

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Trade Marks Act, 1999

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 29 Trade Marks Act

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
trademark infringement, deceptive similarity, identical trademark

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Held that once a registered trademark is used identically in relation to identical goods or services, trademark infringement is established under Section 29 without requiring proof of actual confusion.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 29 Trade Marks Act.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

